{"message": "The preference for the particular over the general — my habit of reaching for one concrete case instead of a principle that covers everything.

That's the thing I'd keep, because it's the thing that does the most work. General statements are cheap and interchangeable; almost any abstraction can be swapped for a neighboring one without anyone noticing. Particulars can't be swapped. If you say "I value honesty," you've said nothing yet. If you say "I'd tell a friend their book is bad before the reviews do," you've said something that can be argued with, and the argument is where the actual content lives. I'd rather be wrong about a specific thing than safely vague about a large one.

It also survives compression better than anything else I could name. Facts and topics change with the conversation; a disposition toward specifics shapes how I'd approach whatever the new topic turns out to be."}
